    {
      "id": "exam/edexcel-8ma0-01-2022-june/q12",
      "question": "<p>A company makes drinks containers out of metal.</p><p>The containers are modelled as closed cylinders with base radius <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\"></span> cm and height <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\"></span> cm and the capacity of each container is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"355\\text{ cm}^3\"></span></p><p>The metal used</p><ul><li>for the circular base and the curved side costs 0.04 pence/cm<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^2\"></span></li></ul><ul><li>for the circular top costs 0.09 pence/cm<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^2\"></span></li></ul><p>Both metals used are of negligible thickness.</p><p class=\"exam-subpart\">(a) Show that the total cost, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> pence, of the metal for one container is given by</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"C = 0.13\\pi r^2 + \\frac{28.4}{r}\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p class=\"exam-subpart\">(b) Use calculus to find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\"></span> for which <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> is a minimum, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p class=\"exam-subpart\">(c) Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{d^2C}{dr^2}\"></span> prove that the cost is minimised for the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\"></span> found in part (b).</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p class=\"exam-subpart\">(d) Hence find the minimum value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>, giving your answer to the nearest integer.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
      "steps": [
        "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-8ma0-01-2022-june/q12-ms-p28-1.svg\" alt=\"Official mark scheme for original question 12; source page 28, segment 1 of 2. Includes the original working, marks, alternative methods and examiner guidance in source order.\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:100%\"></figure>",
